<section class="section intro" id="how">
<div class="section-label">01 / THE LOOP</div>
<div class="section-heading">
<h2>A goal.<br>A loop.<br>
<span>A real result.</span>
</h2>
<div>
<p class="lead">{HERO_TAGLINE}</p>
<p>Kazi is {POSITIONING}. Think of it as {KUBERNETES_LINE}: declare the desired outcome, then close the gap.</p>
<p>You describe the outcome. Your agent drafts the acceptance checks. Kazi observes what is failing, sends the work back to the agent, and checks again.</p>
<p>Tests, exact file contents, a live endpoint: you choose what completion means. Kazi reports success only when every declared check passes.</p>
<a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/blob/main/docs/concept.md">Read the engineering behind it ↗</a>
</div>
</div>
<div class="loop-steps">
<article>
<span>01</span>
<h3>Define done</h3>
<p>Set the goal, its acceptance checks, the allowed scope, and a budget.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span>02</span>
<h3>Close the gap</h3>
<p>Failing checks become the agent’s next assignment. Kazi rechecks after the work.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span>03</span>
<h3>Know the outcome</h3>
<p>Converged, stuck, or over budget. Every stop has a reason you can act on.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section proof" id="proof">
<div class="section-label">02 / EVIDENCE, INCLUDED</div>
<div class="proof-heading">
<h2>Less “trust me.”<br>
<span>More show me.</span>
</h2>
<p>One failing Go test. A real agent run.<br>A result verified by the controller.</p>
</div>
<figure class="terminal">
<div class="terminal-bar">
<span class="terminal-dots">● ● ●</span>
<span>kazi apply · recorded run</span>
<a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/tree/main/priv/examples/hero_cast_demo">Reproduce ↗</a>
</div>
<img src="/proof-loop.gif" alt="Recorded Kazi run: the tests-pass check fails on iteration one, passes on iteration two, and the controller reports CONVERGED." loading="lazy" width="760" height="430">
<figcaption>Actual recorded output from the Kazi repository. <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/blob/main/assets/proof-loop.cast">View the original recording ↗</a>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="evidence-row">
<div>
<span class="small-label">A RECORDED EXAMPLE · v1.64.2</span>
<h3>Existing isn’t the same as exact.</h3>
<p>The goal required VERSION.txt to contain exactly <code>1.0.0</code>. Kazi checked the bytes and drove the run to convergence in 2 iterations.</p>
</div>
<div>
<span class="evidence-number">2</span>
<p>iterations to verified completion<br>
<small>One recorded run, not a performance guarantee.</small>
</p>
<a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/blob/main/docs/dogfood-methodology.md">Inspect the method and evidence ↗</a>
<br>
<a class="text-link" href="/proof">Explore the full proof gallery ↗</a>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section capabilities">
<div class="section-label">03 / BUILT FOR REAL WORK</div>
<h2>More autonomy.<br>
<span>Keep the control.</span>
</h2>
<div class="feature-grid">
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">[✓]</span>
<h3>Your checks hold the line.</h3>
<p>Completion belongs to the controller. Stored evidence makes each result inspectable. The strength of that result depends on the checks you define.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">↗↗</span>
<h3>Parallel work, coordinated.</h3>
<p>Resource leases coordinate overlapping edits. A session bus lets agents exchange updates, while the dashboard makes fleet progress visible.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">⌘</span>
<h3>Keep your coding agent.</h3>
<p>Start with Claude Code. Kazi also supports other CLI harnesses, including Codex and opencode, with documented support tiers.</p>
<a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi#tiered-harness-support-adr-0022">Compare harness support ↗</a>
</article>
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">⊣</span>
<h3>Know when to step in.</h3>
<p>Iteration, time, and token budgets bound the run. Repeated failures surface as stuck, so the next decision returns to you.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">~</span>
<h3>Spend reasoning deliberately.</h3>
<p>Use a stronger model to define the work, then a cheaper one for iterations. Let the orchestrating skill escalate difficult slices. Savings are an intended benefit, not a measured promise.</p>
</article>
<article>
<span class="feature-icon">⌂</span>
<h3>Choose where models run.</h3>
<p>Connect opencode to a local model when your workflow calls for local inference. Data handling still depends on the model, tools, and services you configure.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section getting-started" id="start">
<span id="try">
</span>
<span id="install">
</span>
<div class="section-label">04 / YOUR NEXT FINISHED THING</div>
<div class="start-layout">
<div>
<h2>Stay in your chat.<br>
<span>Give it a loop.</span>
</h2>
<p>Install Kazi once. Add its skill to Claude Code.<br>Then describe the outcome you want to ship.</p>
<p class="requirement">Requires Homebrew and a configured Claude Code installation. The Kazi binary includes its runtime.</p>
<a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi#install">Other platforms and source builds ↗</a>
</div>
<div class="install">
<div class="install-step">
<div>
<span>01</span>
<h3>Install the controller + skill</h3>
</div>
<pre>
<code id="install-code">{INSTALL_CMD}{"\n"}kazi install-skill</code>
</pre>
<button class="copy" data-copy="install-code">Copy commands</button>
</div>
<div class="install-step">
<div>
<span>02</span>
<h3>In Claude Code, define the goal</h3>
</div>
<pre>
<code id="plan-code">/kazi plan "add a /healthz endpoint
that returns 200 ok, with a test,
deployed"</code>
</pre>
<button class="copy" data-copy="plan-code">Copy prompt</button>
</div>
<div class="install-step">
<div>
<span>03</span>
<h3>Review the checks. Start the loop.</h3>
</div>
<pre>
<code id="apply-code">/kazi apply</code>
</pre>
<button class="copy" data-copy="apply-code">Copy command</button>
</div>
<p class="small-note">Prefer plain English? Say <strong>{INVOCATION_PHRASE}</strong> in Claude Code after installing the skill.</p>
<p class="small-note">The skill installs locally. Kazi drives your coding agent; it does not bundle one.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section faq">
<div class="section-label">05 / BEFORE YOU RUN</div>
<div class="faq-layout">
<h2>Good questions.<br>
<span>Straight answers.</span>
</h2>
<div>
<details>
<summary>Is Kazi another coding agent?</summary>
<p>Kazi is a reconciliation controller around the coding agent you already use. The agent edits code. Kazi evaluates the checks and decides whether more work is needed.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Does a passing run mean my software is perfect?</summary>
<p>A passing run means all declared acceptance checks passed. Missing or weak checks leave gaps. Review the proposed checks before applying a goal, and include the deployed behavior when that matters.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>What happens when the agent cannot finish?</summary>
<p>Kazi can stop as stuck when failures repeat, or over budget when a run reaches its configured limits. You can inspect the evidence, refine the goal, or change the approach.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Is it free to use?</summary>
<p>Kazi is open source under Apache-2.0. Your coding agent, model provider, and infrastructure may have their own costs. <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/blob/main/LICENSE">Read the license.</a>
</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Can it check a deployed service?</summary>
<p>Yes. Acceptance predicates can probe a live endpoint. Deployment requires your own configured commands, tools, and access. Include the live check in the goal when production behavior defines done.</p>
</details>
<details>
<summary>Where do I report bugs or contribute?</summary>
<p>Use <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/issues">GitHub Issues</a> for bugs and <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi/discussions">Discussions</a> for questions. The repository includes setup instructions, architecture decisions, and reproducible examples.</p>
</details>
</div>
</div>
</section>
